---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import { site } from "../config/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Accessibility at Sift"
  description="Sift's accessibility goals, including a WCAG 2.2 Level AA target and a direct way to report barriers."
  path="/accessibility"
>
  <PageHero
    title="Sift should be usable when the decision matters."
    description="We aim to make the Sift website understandable, navigable, and robust for disabled visitors and people using assistive technologies."
    compact
  />

  <section class="content-section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      {site.accessibilityAuditDate && (
        <div class="legal-meta" aria-label="Accessibility review details">
          <span><strong>Last reviewed:</strong> {site.accessibilityAuditDate}</span>
          <span><strong>Target:</strong> WCAG 2.2 Level AA</span>
        </div>
      )}
      <h2>Our accessibility target</h2>
      <p>
        We aim to conform to the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 Level AA. This is a working
        standard for design, implementation, testing, and ongoing improvement—not a claim that the website has been
        independently certified or is free of every accessibility barrier.
      </p>

      <h2>What we design and test for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Semantic landmarks, headings, lists, links, and controls that expose meaningful structure.</li>
        <li>Keyboard navigation with a visible focus indicator and a way to skip repeated navigation.</li>
        <li>Readable color contrast, with allergy or risk information never communicated by color alone.</li>
        <li>Content that reflows at narrow widths and remains usable when text is enlarged or the page is zoomed.</li>
        <li>Descriptive text alternatives for meaningful images and appropriately hidden decorative graphics.</li>
        <li>Reduced-motion support for visitors who prefer less animation.</li>
        <li>Plain, direct language and predictable navigation across product, safety, support, and legal pages.</li>
        <li>Touch targets sized for comfortable use where practical.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Compatibility</h2>
      <p>
        Accessibility can vary with the browser, operating system, assistive technology, and personal settings in
        use. We focus testing on current, standards-based browsers and common keyboard and screen-reader workflows,
        while continuing to address problems found in other configurations.
      </p>

      <h2>Report an accessibility barrier</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}?subject=Accessibility%20feedback`}>{site.supportEmail}</a> with
        “Accessibility feedback” in the subject line. If you can, include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The page address or feature where the problem occurred.</li>
        <li>What you were trying to do and what happened instead.</li>
        <li>Your browser, device, operating system, and assistive technology.</li>
        <li>The format or accommodation that would help you access the information.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout callout--warning">
        <strong>Do not include sensitive health information, allergy profile details, or other private data unless our support team asks for specific information and explains why it is needed.</strong>
      </div>
      <p>
        If a barrier prevents you from accessing safety, privacy, or support information, tell us what information
        you need and we will work to provide an accessible alternative.
      </p>

      <h2>Third-party services</h2>
      <p>
        Sift may link to services we do not control, such as an app marketplace or an email provider. Their
        accessibility is governed by their own products and policies, but feedback about an external step in the
        Sift journey is still useful to us.
      </p>

      <h2>Ongoing work</h2>
      <p>
        Accessibility is part of product quality, not a one-time checklist. We use reported barriers, manual review,
        and automated checks to guide improvements, with priority given to issues that block access to core product,
        safety, support, privacy, or legal information.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
